# Copy to .env and fill in. Read by docker compose.
#   cp .env.example .env

# --- database (required) --------------------------------------------------

# Password for the bundled Postgres. Any long random string:
#   openssl rand -base64 24
# Changing it after first boot does NOT change the existing database — the
# volume keeps the original password. Reset with `docker compose down -v`.
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=

# Only if you point the app at a Postgres you already run. Left unset, compose
# builds the URL from POSTGRES_* and the bundled `postgres` service.
# DATABASE_URL=postgres://user:pass@host:5432/craftmate
# POSTGRES_USER=craftmate
# POSTGRES_DB=craftmate

# Host interface and port Postgres is published on, for a GUI client. Bound to
# loopback by default; 5434 rather than 5432, which a local install often holds.
# POSTGRES_BIND=127.0.0.1
# POSTGRES_PORT=5434

# --- backend ---------------------------------------------------------------

# Only if you point the backend at a Postgres you already run. Left unset,
# compose builds the SQLAlchemy URL from POSTGRES_* and the bundled service.
# API_DATABASE_URL=postgresql+psycopg://user:pass@host:5432/craftmate

# Host interface and port the backend is published on. Loopback by default:
# the Electron app on this machine reaches it here, a browser never does
# because the dashboard proxies /api to it inside the compose network.
# API_BIND=127.0.0.1
# API_PORT=8100

# Same for the agent server (MCP over HTTP at /mcp). The app's harness and
# any MCP client connect here with an x-api-key header.
# AGENT_BIND=127.0.0.1
# AGENT_PORT=8484

# Where the dashboard proxies /api. Inside compose this is the api service.
# MEELU_API_URL=http://api:8000

# --- dashboard -------------------------------------------------------

# Host interface and port for the published dashboard port. Loopback-only by
# default so a VPS doesn't expose the app unproxied. Behind the Caddy overlay
# leave these alone — Caddy reaches the dashboard over the internal network.
# DASHBOARD_BIND=127.0.0.1
# DASHBOARD_PORT=3000

# --- Unsplash image search (optional) -------------------------------------

# Powers the "search Unsplash" half of the image picker on content posts.
# Without it, uploading still works and the picker says search is unavailable.
# Create an app at https://unsplash.com/oauth/applications and copy its Access
# Key (the Secret Key is not needed — we only read).
#
# New apps are in demo mode: 50 searches/hour. Applying for production raises
# it. Meelu already does the two things their guidelines require for that
# approval — crediting the photographer with the UTM links, and pinging the
# download endpoint when a photo is chosen.
# UNSPLASH_ACCESS_KEY=

# --- VPS with HTTPS (docker-compose.prod.yml) -----------------------------

# Domain whose A/AAAA record already points at this host. Caddy gets the cert.
# MEELU_DOMAIN=meelu.example.com

# ── Search Console (app only) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The OAuth client the Search Console connector authorises against. This is the
# DEPLOYMENT's client, not a user's credential and not a platform data key: it
# buys nothing and grants nothing on its own. Its whole job is to let a user
# connect their own Search Console without first creating a Google Cloud
# project, which is the step that made the old service-account setup a
# three-parter with the middle step in Google's UI.
#
# Google's installed-app model explicitly does not treat a Desktop-app client's
# secret as confidential — PKCE is what binds the exchange — which is why this
# can ship inside the app and why no backend is involved. The per-user thing,
# the refresh token this client obtains, never leaves the user's device and is
# never seen by any Meelu server.
#
# Create it in Google Cloud → APIs & Services → Credentials → Create
# credentials → OAuth client ID → Application type "Desktop app", and enable
# the Search Console API on the same project. Check the scope tier while you
# are there: webmasters.readonly must appear under "Non-sensitive scopes" on
# the consent screen. If Google files it as sensitive instead, the project is
# capped at 100 users for its lifetime and refresh tokens die every 7 days
# until verification clears — worth knowing before you ship it.
# MEELU_GSC_CLIENT_ID=
# MEELU_GSC_CLIENT_SECRET=

# ── Google Analytics (app only) ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# The OAuth client the GA4 connector authorises against. Same deal as the
# Search Console client above: this is the DEPLOYMENT's client, not a user's
# credential, and it reads nothing on its own. It exists so a user can connect
# their own GA4 property without first standing up a Google Cloud project.
#
# It is a SECOND client rather than a reuse of the Search Console one, and
# deliberately so. analytics.readonly is a sensitive scope; webmasters.readonly
# is not. One client carrying both scopes drags Search Console into GA's
# verification regime — the unverified 100-user cap and refresh tokens that die
# every 7 days — so a GA review would break search-data syncs that have nothing
# to do with it. Two clients keep the two failure modes apart.
#
# Create it in Google Cloud → APIs & Services → Credentials → Create
# credentials → OAuth client ID → Application type "Desktop app", and enable
# both the Google Analytics Admin API (it lists the properties the user can
# pick from) and the Google Analytics Data API (it runs the report) on the same
# project.
# MEELU_GA_CLIENT_ID=
# MEELU_GA_CLIENT_SECRET=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Crawling
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Every crawl runs in the app, on your own machine and IP, and each website is
# its own task. The task limits below are per crawl; MEELU_CRAWL_MAX_OUTBOUND
# is the ceiling across all of them at once, so crawling your own site and
# three competitors together still opens one bounded set of connections rather
# than four independent ones.
#
# MEELU_CRAWL_MAX_OUTBOUND=12
# MEELU_CRAWL_PER_ORIGIN=6
# MEELU_API_CONNECTIONS=8
#
# Pages fetched at once within one crawl. Your own site gets the first number,
# somebody else's site the second — a competitor is a guest visit, not a load
# test. Both are still gated by MEELU_CRAWL_MAX_OUTBOUND above.
# MEELU_CRAWL_CONCURRENCY=8
# MEELU_CRAWL_RIVAL_CONCURRENCY=3
#
# Postgres connections the api container keeps. The sum across every
# container has to stay under the server's max_connections (100).
# DATABASE_POOL_MAX=20

